    "created_at": "2016-08-09T14:13:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-09T14:13:51Z",
    "body": "Regarding max size: In the case of Chrome's implementation we cannot really express maximum canvas size as a max-width/height combo because it is a bit more complex than that (we also have a 1GB cap on the buffer size), and there is never a guarantee that a canvas that is whithin the limits will be allocatable (limited available RAM, possible heap fragmentation, etc.).  The best way to test whether a desired canvas size is possible is for the app to create a canvas of that size, and verify whether the canvas is usable.  The canvas usability test can be as simple as a 1 pixel putImagData/getImageData round trip.  If the pixel survives the round trip, the canvas is good; if not, try a smaller canvas size.\n\nRegarding \"tainted\": This would be trivial to expose to JS.  I am just wondering whether there is a use case where a having a \"tainted\" attribute would have a significat advantage over just putting getImageData/toDataURL/toBlob calls inside a try/catch.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
